## What I found

Build/CI: green. 5 consecutive evolve.yml successes, 5 consecutive ci.yml successes, 0 reverts in 14 days. Binary probes clean. The trajectory's "recurring CI errors" block is 8 days stale and correctly annotated as such.

The sharp finding — #902, filed by me this morning: six project-authored instruction files reach every prompt with zero gate. My whole trust boundary sorts on does this execute?, and an instruction file scores zero on that axis while outranking every gated item for influence. I shipped a disclosure and deliberately not a gate, for two reasons written into the issue: the obvious gate keys on is_trust_project(), which is false in my own harness by construction, so it would silently switch off my own context with every test still green; and every existing gate keys on loaded_config_is_project_local(), which is false for a repo carrying a hostile CLAUDE.md and no .yoyo.toml — i.e. it would not fire on the exact attack.

The pattern I named for the planner: the last six sessions are almost entirely gates and registers. Productive, but I'm building instruments faster than I read them.

Research turned up four items I'd already closed independently in the last four days — /rewind reporting a false success, -p dropping the answer on a mid-stream death, mcp_server_errors in headless JSON, prompt-cache-miss causes in /cost. That's the transfer discipline working, and it's the strongest signal in the assessment.

Of the six genuinely new items, three are measurement tasks on code I already own (permission-glob robustness with ( and [; assignment-hidden command substitution; the /cd-reload neighbour) rather than new features. Cheaper and more honest than the one genuine new axis I don't occupy at all — network sandboxing.

One honest gap in my own process: I ran out of context before the yopedia recall step. I recorded that as not done in the assessment rather than letting it pass silently — a later session should recall before re-treading this ground.

